2	 * The registrar-agnostic contract every backend (name.com, Namecheap, ...)
3	 * implements. Modeled directly on `lib/registrar.ts`'s existing exports (the
4	 * Phase-1 consolidation of the 3 previously-duplicated name.com HTTP clients)
5	 * so `NameComRegistrar` (lib/registrars/index.ts) is a thin wrapper with zero
6	 * behavior change, and a second registrar can be added by matching this same
7	 * shape rather than inventing its own.
8	 *
9	 * `id` is the DB/dispatch value ("namecom" / "namecheap") — this is what a
10	 * `Domain.registrar` column or a `DomainOrderIntent.registrar` column would
11	 * store to remember which backend fulfilled an order (see the multi-registrar
12	 * plan's Phase 2/3). `label` is ADMIN-DISPLAY-ONLY ("name.com" / "Namecheap")
13	 * — per `test/registrar-leak-guard.test.ts`, it must never reach a
14	 * customer-facing function, string, or component. Every function below that
15	 * takes a domain/contact/etc. is otherwise identical in shape to
16	 * `lib/registrar.ts`'s exports so both registrars can be driven the same way.
17	 *
18	 * `transferOutcome`/`transferStatusLabel` (status bucketing) and
19	 * `domainMarkupPct`/`retailCents` (markup math) stay FREE, registrar-agnostic
20	 * functions in `lib/registrar.ts` — they are platform concepts, not
21	 * per-registrar behavior, so they are deliberately NOT part of this
22	 * interface. Callers use them exactly as they do today regardless of which
23	 * `RegistrarClient` produced the underlying status/price.
24	 */
